STRUCTURED_RUNTIME_STATE_ADDENDUM = (
    "\n\nRuntime variables inside every `python` tool call:\n"
    "- `current_frame` is a lightweight frame view for the latest environment state.\n"
    "- `current_frame` exposes only `.ascii`, `.step`, `.level`, `.shape`, and `.segmentation`.\n"
    "- `current_frame.ascii` is a single newline-delimited string containing the latest board rendered with the letter-coded ARC color symbols.\n"
    "- `current_frame.segmentation` parses the board into objects. It returns `{'nodes': [...], 'adjacency_list': [...]}`.\n"
    "- Each node in `segmentation['nodes']` is one 4-connected same-color object with: `id` (index, ordered top-most-left-most), `color` (ARC color character), `hash` (a signature of the object's color and shape that ignores its position -- equal hashes mean the same object regardless of where it is, so use it to track an object across frames or to spot multiple identical objects in one frame), `shape_hash` (the same signature with color left out, so it matches same-shaped objects of different colors and survives a recolor), `pixels` (cell count), `bbox` (`[min_row, min_col, max_row, max_col]`), `centroid` (`[row, col]` mean position), `boundary` (clockwise outer-perimeter corner points as `[row, col]`), and `children` (ids of objects fully enclosed by this one).\n"
    "- Read an object's position from `bbox` and `centroid` rather than deriving it from `boundary`. `centroid` can fall outside the object for a ring or L-shape, so use it to compare positions, not as a guaranteed click target.\n"
    "- `segmentation['adjacency_list']` is a list of `[i, j]` node-id pairs whose objects share an edge.\n"
    
    "- `current_frame.step` is the current environment step count.\n"
    "- `current_frame.level` is the current level number.\n"
    "- `current_frame.shape` is a `(rows, cols)` tuple.\n"
    "- The raw numeric grid is intentionally not exposed. Use `current_frame.segmentation` as your primary view of the board -- objects, colors, shapes, containment, adjacency, and cross-frame object hashes. To read a small, specific region use `current_frame.crop(bbox)` or `current_frame.window(row, col)`; do not scan the whole board through `.ascii`.\n"

    "- `history` is a chronological list of action/frame snapshots.\n"
    "- `history` is a Python list of objects, not a dict.\n"
    "- Each history entry exposes only `.action` and `.frame`; entries are not subscriptable like `entry['action']`.\n"
    "- Each `history[i].frame` is the frame after `history[i].action`; each frame exposes only `.ascii`, `.step`, `.level`, `.shape`, and `.segmentation`.\n"
    "- Important history semantics: when `history` is non-empty, `history[-1].frame` is the same latest/post-action board as `current_frame`. It is not the previous board. To inspect the state before the latest action, use `previous_frame` or `history[-2].frame` when available.\n"
    "- `previous_frame` is the frame before the most recent real environment action, or `None` if no previous frame is available.\n"
    "- `last_action` is the most recent real environment action name/display, or `None` before any real action.\n"
    "- `last_action_frame` is the post-action frame for `last_action`; it matches `current_frame` after a real action.\n"
    "- `transitions` is a chronological list of actual action transitions, excluding the initial seeded frame. Each transition exposes `.action`, `.before_frame`, `.after_frame`, `.frame` (alias of `.after_frame`), and `.result`.\n"
    "- `last_transition` is `transitions[-1]` or `None`, and it covers exactly one action. Its `.result` mirrors `last_action_result`; older transitions may have an empty `.result`. For before/after diffs, compare `last_transition.before_frame` to `last_transition.after_frame`; do not compare `current_frame` to `history[-1].frame`.\n"
    "- `last_batch` covers the whole most recent `action(...)` call, including one made in an earlier turn: `.diff()` and `.track()` compare the frame before the first action in the list to the frame after the last one, and `.steps` is the per-action transition list for that call. It is `None` only before any action has been taken.\n"
    "- When you pass `action(...)` several actions at once, `last_transition` is only the final one. Reading it to judge a multi-action probe hides everything the earlier actions did and will report no change even when the probe worked. Use `last_batch.diff()` for the batch as a whole, or `last_batch.steps[i].diff()` to attribute the change to one action in it.\n"
    "- `last_action_result` is the persisted result dict from the most recent `action(...)` call. It remains available across later Python inspection calls that do not call `action(...)`, and is `{}` before any action result exists. Read transition metadata from fields/keys such as `last_action_result['board_changed']`, `last_action_result['done']`, `last_action_result['level_completed']`, `last_action_result['game_over']`, `last_action_result['run_complete']`, `last_action_result['reward']`, and `last_action_result['valid_actions']`.\n"
    "- `diff_frames(before_frame, after_frame)` summarizes what changed between two frames so you do not have to write diff code yourself. `last_transition.diff()` is the same thing for the most recent action.\n"
    "- `diff_frames` returns `changed` (bool), `changed_cells` (count), `bbox` (`[min_row, min_col, max_row, max_col]` covering every change, or `None`), `shape`, `transitions` (per color-pair counts with sample cells), `regions` (clusters of changed cells as `bbox` + `cells`), and `objects`.\n"
    "- `objects` holds `moved` (each with `from`, `to`, `delta`, `color`, `pixels`), `appeared`, `disappeared`, a `stable` count of objects that did not move, and `background_reshaped`. An object that changed shape or color appears as a `disappeared`/`appeared` pair rather than as `moved`.\n"
    "- Large background regions are excluded from `objects` because their shape changes whenever anything on top of them moves; `background_reshaped` reports that separately. `background_colors` lists their colors.\n"
    "- Every `diff_frames` list is capped, and each has a matching `*_total` count so you can tell a truncated summary from a complete one. Pass larger `max_transitions`, `max_regions`, `max_objects`, or `max_cells_per_transition` when you need more detail, and `objects=False` to skip object matching.\n"

    "\nPerception helpers -- prefer these over writing your own scanning code:\n"
    "- `track_objects(before_frame, after_frame)` follows objects across two frames even when they change, where `diff_frames` reports only exact shape matches. `last_transition.track()` is the same for the most recent action. It returns `matched` (each with `before_id`, `after_id`, `by` -- `'shape'` for an exact match or `'position'` for one inferred from staying put at a similar size -- plus `colors`, `pixels`, `from`, `to`, `delta`, and `changed` naming which of moved/color/size/shape differ), `unmatched_before` and `unmatched_after` for things genuinely gone or genuinely new, each with a `*_total` count, and `stable`. Use it when something you are tracking is growing, shrinking, or changing color as it moves.\n"
    "- `find_hud(current_frame)` computes which edge elements look like HUD instead of you judging it by eye. `candidates` holds each suspected element with `kind` (`'bar'` for one long thin component, `'segmented_bar'` for a strip of repeated identical blocks), `edge`, `bbox`, `colors`, `segments`, and `span_fraction`. `interior_bbox` is the board with those bands trimmed off -- treat that as the playfield and confirm a candidate by checking that actions change it while the interior stays still.\n"
    "- Always read `candidates`, not just `interior_bbox`. Before you treat any element near an edge as a gameplay mechanic -- an energy meter to fill, a progress bar to charge, a row of pieces to click -- check whether it is already flagged there. A flagged bar that changes on its own each step is a timer, and no amount of clicking will drive it.\n"
    "- `find_background(current_frame, other_frames=[previous_frame])` returns the likely background `color` with the evidence for it: `fraction` of the board, `components` (how many pieces that color forms), `largest_component`, `color_counts`, and `stable_fraction` -- how much of that color held steady across `other_frames`. A color that covers most of the board in one component and does not change is background; one that covers a lot but keeps changing is gameplay.\n"
    "- `find_symmetry(current_frame, bbox=None)` tests `left_right`, `top_bottom`, `rotation_180`, `rotation_90`, `transpose`, and `anti_transpose`. Each reports `exact`, `mismatches`, `compared`, and `score`; `symmetric` lists the ones that hold exactly. Pass a node's `bbox` to ask about one object. A high `score` that is not `exact` means nearly symmetric, which is often the goal state, and `mismatches` locates how far off it is. Pass `ignore_colors` to ignore the background or a piece sitting on top.\n"
    "- `path_between(current_frame, [row, col], goal, blocked_colors=[...])` is a shortest-path search you do not have to write. `goal` may be one `[row, col]` or a list, in which case the nearest reachable one wins. Give `blocked_colors` for walls, or `passable_colors` to allow only certain colors; start and goal are always allowed. It returns `found`, `steps`, `moves` (`UP`/`DOWN`/`LEFT`/`RIGHT`, where `UP` is a decreasing row), `runs` (the same `moves` collapsed to `[direction, count]` pairs, a compact way to read the route), `path`, `goal_reached`, `explored`, and `reason` when there is no route. Add `diagonal=True` for diagonal moves.\n"
    "- `action_effects()` tallies, for the current level, which actions have actually changed the board and which never have. It returns `actions` and `click_targets` (each with `attempts`, `changed`, and `dead`), the `dead_actions` and `dead_click_targets` shortlists, and `wasted_actions` counting how many actions have already gone into things now known to be dead. Clicks are grouped by what sat under the cursor -- color, size, and shape -- so clicking a second object of the same kind counts against the same verdict.\n"
    "- Treat anything `dead` as unavailable for the rest of the level: an action tried repeatedly with zero board changes is not going to start working, and every retry costs score. Check `action_effects()` before repeating an action or clicking another object of a kind you have already tried, and pick a genuinely different probe instead. Verdicts reset per level, so re-test after the level changes.\n"
    "- `current_frame.crop(bbox)` renders one region as ASCII with row and column numbers on it, so you can read coordinates instead of counting. `current_frame.window(row, col, radius=3)` does the same for the area around one cell. Both clip to the board, and `labels=False` drops the numbering. Use these instead of slicing `.ascii` by hand.\n"
    "- `valid_actions` is the current list of valid action names.\n"
    "- Call `action(actions)` to execute one or more real environment actions from Python.\n"
    "- Pass `action(actions)` a list like `['LEFT']` or `[{'action': 'MOUSE', 'row': 4, 'col': 7}]`.\n"
    "- One action usually returns one frame, but a single action can result in a short multi-frame animation.\n"
    "- After `action(actions)` returns, `current_frame`, `previous_frame`, `history`, `transitions`, `valid_actions`, and `last_action_result` are refreshed.\n"
)

